title: "AI Act: what really changes on 2 December 2026"
lang: "en"
publishedAt: "2026-11-27"
category: "Software Development & AI"
excerpt: "AI transparency: in force since 2 August 2026. 2 December only changes machine-readable marking and two bans. A checklist for your Odoo."
---

2 December is five days away. Several commentators present it as a general postponement of AI transparency. That is wrong.

If your website has an AI chat, or if your teams have a model write texts for them, you are already affected. This article lists what to check in Odoo and on your website. It is not a substitute for legal advice.

## What the timeline says, without shortcuts

Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, the "Digital Omnibus" on AI, 
was published in the Official Journal on 24 July 2026 and has been in force since 27 July 2026
. It mainly postpones the high-risk rules: 
December 2027 for Annex III systems, August 2028 for those in Annex I
.

Transparency has not moved. 
The rules in Article 50 have applied since 2 August 2026.


2 December is a narrow exception. 
The extension covers only the machine-readable marking required by Article 50(2), and only for generative systems already on the market before 2 August 2026.
 This marking is the responsibility of the providers of models and tools, not yours.

Two new bans also take effect on that date: 
AI designed to produce child sexual abuse material and non-consensual intimate images, such as "nudifier" tools
. They target the providers of these tools first and foremost.

In practical terms, 2 December gives you no extra time to disclose that a chat is an AI.

## The Odoo and website checklist

### 1. Live chat

Since version 19, Odoo has offered 
an AI agent connected to Live Chat, which can also generate leads
. The Odoo 20 documentation describes 
an agent that answers common questions, asks for clarification and decides when to hand over to a human
.

On paper, Article 50(1) weighs first on the provider: 
it must design the system so that people know they are talking to an AI
. But you are the one who names the agent, writes its greeting and decides what the visitor sees.

What to check:
- Open your website in a private browsing window. From the very first message, can the visitor tell they are talking to an AI?
- Use the channel's welcome message. 
It is configured in the Options tab of the Live Chat channel.
 One clear sentence is enough.
- Do not rely on it being obvious. 
According to an analysis of the Commission's guidelines, the exception for "obvious" cases must be read restrictively, and support chatbots do not benefit from it.
 
A smooth bot with a human tone does not pass this test.

- Do not give the agent a human first name without an AI notice.

If a team member reviews every reply before it is sent, the reading is different. 
The visitor is then talking to a human using a tool, and Article 50(1) is unlikely to apply.
 This source is an analysis, not an official text: decide with your legal adviser.

### 2. Emails and automated agents

In the sources I consulted, I found no general obligation to label every email written with AI assistance. Odoo allows AI to be used in email templates, and a team member who reviews and sends the message remains its author.

The case to watch is different. With Odoo 20, 
agents run in automated and scheduled actions, not only in a chat window
. An agent that replies to a customer by email on its own behaves like a chatbot. My reading, as a precaution: flag it in the signature or the first message. This point is not settled by the sources I have seen.

### 3. Automatic summaries

A summary of a ticket, meeting or discussion thread read by your teams is not a published text. The guidelines specifically exclude 
the chatbot summary available only to the individual user
. No label is needed.

This settles neither GDPR nor quality. A wrong summary is still wrong. Keep a human in the loop before any decision.

### 4. Texts and images published on the website

Odoo lets you 
create or rewrite website pages with AI
 and, according to its 20 documentation, 
generate images with AI
.

Two separate rules apply:
- **Text.** 
A label is required when the text is published to inform the public on matters of public interest.
 A product page or a blog article about your trade does not generally fall into this category. However, the guidelines cite 
health, consumer safety or sustainability claims
 as an area requiring vigilance.
- **Images and videos.** 
A deepfake requires a visible notice.
 A realistic visual showing a fake event, a fake customer or a fake person is covered.

The exception exists for texts. It requires 
a genuine substantive review and a person who bears editorial responsibility
. A simple spelling correction 
is not enough
.

### 5. The evidence

Record who reviews sensitive texts and who is accountable for them. 
Also keep screenshots of your notices and update your privacy policy.
 If a customer raises a question or you face an inspection, this file is what counts.

## What 2 December changes for you

For most companies using Odoo, very little. The work lies with the providers. 
The Odoo 19 documentation names Gemini and OpenAI as providers for the AI module.
 They, along with the publishers of generative tools, are the ones who must mark the content produced.

Two caveats:
- 
Tools launched after 2 August had to be compliant from the start.
 The extension benefits only older tools.
- If you develop a generative tool yourself and offer it to third parties, you may become a provider. Ask the question beforehand, not afterwards.

On 2 December, ask your AI providers what they have planned for marking. A written answer is worth more than a verbal promise.

## What is not for right now

High risk, for its part, really has been postponed. 
Annex III systems, such as recruitment or staff evaluation, now fall under 2 December 2027.
 If you use AI to sort applications, this task awaits you, but not this week.

The Article 5 prohibitions, meanwhile, have applied 
since 2 February 2025
.

## In practice, this week

1. Test your chat as a visitor would.
2. Add the AI notice to the welcome message.
3. List your agents that write to customers without review.
4. Identify the AI-generated texts and images published on your website.
5. Write to your AI providers about marking.

